# Quick Boundaries

- `src/core` — pure TypeScript shared kernel; no SDKs, no React, no imports from infrastructure/features/app/lib
- `src/infrastructure` — implements ports, may use SDKs; feature-agnostic (never imports `src/features`)
- `src/features/<feature>` — one vertical slice: domain, use case, schema, api hooks, components; never imports another feature
- `src/app/api/**/route.ts` — auth + rate limit + Zod validation + DI (only place to instantiate adapters)
- `src/lib` — HTTP client, env, logger, rate limit, error mapping; no feature or infrastructure imports
- UI — calls server via React Query hooks only

# Non-negotiables

- Secrets go through `serverEnv()` in `src/lib/env.ts`. `process.env` elsewhere is a lint error.
- Logging goes through `logger` in `src/lib/logger.ts`. `console.*` elsewhere is a lint error.
- Domain errors extend `DomainError` and carry `status`; Route Handlers always end in `handleRouteError(error, context)`.
- Route Handlers that touch a metered or paid resource call `enforceRateLimit` first.
- Wrap Route Handlers in `routeHandler(name, fn)` — it supplies the request id and the try/catch.
- Never do third-party I/O inside `IUnitOfWork.transaction`; enqueue a job instead.
- Payment state changes only in the Stripe webhook handler. Never grant access from a checkout redirect or a request body.
- Client components never import `@/lib/env`, `@/features/auth/auth`, `@/features/auth/session`, or anything under `@/infrastructure`.

**These are enforced by `eslint.config.mjs`.** If `pnpm lint` rejects an import,
the fix is the design, not the rule.
